Aurora17 / FIFA 17 on CrossOver 26.3 — CNG and PKCS#12 support in crypt32
========================================================================

Before you build: gnutls must be switched on
--------------------------------------------
This is not optional and it is not obvious.  gnutls is compiled out of the
CrossOver 26.3 tree -- config.h has SONAME_LIBGNUTLS undefined -- so the whole
unix half of crypt32 builds to stubs returning STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND, which the PE
side reports as "call not implemented".  Everything below will compile and do
nothing.  It is a configure result, not source, so it cannot travel in a patch:

    # in your build64 directory
    sed -i '' 's|/\* #undef SONAME_LIBGNUTLS \*/|#define SONAME_LIBGNUTLS "libgnutls.30.dylib"|' \
        include/config.h
    ln -s /opt/homebrew/include/gnutls include/gnutls

Only the headers are needed.  The library itself is opened by name at runtime, and
CrossOver 26.3 already ships libgnutls.30.dylib in its lib64 directory.

What this is for
----------------
.NET 8 does all of its certificate work through CNG. Wine's CNG support is thin
enough that Aurora17's launcher could neither create its own certificate nor read
back the one already on disk, and failed with "CryptographicException: Success."
— which is what .NET prints when a call fails without setting an error code.

Three gaps, each of which only became visible once the one before it was closed:

  1. CertSetCertificateContextProperty ignored property 78,
     CERT_NCRYPT_KEY_HANDLE_PROP_ID, which is how .NET attaches a freshly
     generated CNG key to a new certificate.
  2. PFXExportCertStoreEx was a two-line stub returning FALSE. It is what
     X509Certificate2.Export(Pfx) calls.
  3. PFXImportCertStore ignored PKCS12_ALWAYS_CNG_KSP, so a certificate imported
     by .NET came back with no usable private key.

Full write-up, with the evidence for each: BUGS.md section 13.

Two things worth knowing before reading the diff
------------------------------------------------
  * For property 78, pvData IS the handle, not a pointer to it. Wine's
    neighbouring CERT_KEY_PROV_HANDLE_PROP_ID uses the opposite convention.
  * Wine's ncrypt records the provider on the key as
    NCRYPT_PROVIDER_HANDLE_PROPERTY, and .NET frees that handle when it disposes
    the CngKey. So import_key_cng must NOT free the provider it hands over —
    doing so crashed the caller with c0000005 inside its own image. See
    BUGS.md 14.5.

Applying it
-----------
Apply AFTER crossover-26.3-fifa17-online.patch, which touches dlls/crypt32/pfx.c
as well:

    tar xzf crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz
    cd sources/wine
    patch -p1 < crossover-26.3-fifa17-rosetta.patch
    patch -p1 < crossover-26.3-fifa17-online.patch
    patch -p1 < crossover-26.3-fifa17-audio.patch
    patch -p1 < crossover-26.3-fifa17-cng.patch

